---

<BaseLayout
  title="Architecture"
  description="How this site is wired together: the build pipeline, the visualization layers, and the recipes for adding a visualization, a post or a project."
  noindex
>
  <div class="container shell">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="toc-title">On this page</p>
      <ol>
        {SECTIONS.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a></li>)}
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <article class="doc">
      <header class="doc-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Internals</p>
        <h1>How this site is built</h1>
        <p class="lede muted">
          A static Astro site with React islands. Prose lives in MDX, algorithms live in plain
          TypeScript, and the two meet in a React component that the MDX imports and that hydrates
          only when it scrolls into view. Everything below is what the code does today:
          <span class="n">22</span> visualization pages, <span class="n">21</span> visualization
          components, <span class="n">5</span> shared building blocks and
          <span class="n">23</span> algorithm modules.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section id="overview">
        <h2><span class="num">01</span> The short version</h2>
        <p>Four ideas explain almost every file under <code>src/</code>.</p>
        <ol class="big-list">
          <li>
            <strong>Content is data.</strong> Every page of prose is a file in
            <code>src/content/</code>. A Zod schema in <code>src/content.config.ts</code> validates
            its frontmatter at build time, so a typo in a date fails the build instead of the page.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Routes are generated, never hand-written.</strong> Three dynamic routes
            (<code>[...id].astro</code>) walk their collection and emit one HTML file per entry.
            You never touch <code>src/pages/</code> to publish something.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Algorithms are pure TypeScript.</strong> Nothing under <code>src/lib/</code>
            imports React — that holds across all 23 modules. They take input and return either a
            result or an array of <em>steps</em>. That is what makes them testable, and reusable by
            more than one page.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Interactivity is opt-in, per component.</strong> The site ships as static HTML
            with no JavaScript. A <code>client:visible</code> directive on one component is the only
            thing that loads a React bundle — for that component alone, when the reader reaches it.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section id="pipeline">
        <h2><span class="num">02</span> Build pipeline</h2>
        <p>
          <code>npm run build</code> runs this once per entry and writes plain HTML into
          <code>dist/</code>. Nothing runs on a server afterwards.
        </p>

