          </svg>
        </div>

        <h3>What each stage does</h3>
        <dl class="steps">
          <div>
            <dt><code>src/content.config.ts</code></dt>
            <dd>
              Declares the three collections with Astro's <code>glob()</code> loader. The
              <strong>file name becomes the entry <code>id</code></strong>, which becomes the URL
              slug. The Zod schema is the single source of truth for what frontmatter is legal.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>src/lib/content.ts</code></dt>
            <dd>
              Three thin accessors — <code>getPosts()</code>, <code>getProjects()</code>,
              <code>getVisualizations()</code> — that apply sorting and the draft rule in one place,
              so no page has to remember either.
              <pre class="code"><code>{draftCode}</code></pre>
              Posts and visualizations sort newest first; projects sort <code>featured</code> first,
              then by date.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>src/pages/**/[...id].astro</code></dt>
            <dd>
              <code>getStaticPaths()</code> returns one <code>params.id</code> per entry, and
              <code>render(entry)</code> turns the MDX body into a <code>&lt;Content /&gt;</code>
              component. That is the entire route file — all three look like this.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>src/layouts/</code></dt>
            <dd>
              <code>BaseLayout</code> owns <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>: title, canonical URL, RSS and
              sitemap links, and an inline script that applies the saved theme
              <em>before first paint</em> so there is no flash. <code>ArticleLayout</code> wraps it
              with the title block, date, tag row, a <code>back</code> link and the
              <code>.prose</code> container.
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>

      <section id="layers">
        <h2><span class="num">03</span> The four layers</h2>
        <p>
          A visualization page is four files, each depending only on the one below it. Read bottom
          to top and it is just: maths → picture → words → URL.
        </p>

        <ol class="layers">
          <li>
            <span class="tag lay">route</span>
            <div>
              <code>src/pages/visualize/[...id].astro</code>
              <p>Generic, already exists. You never edit it to add a visualization.</p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tag lay">prose</span>
            <div>
              <code>src/content/visualizations/merge-sort.mdx</code>
              <p>
                Frontmatter plus explanation, one <code>import</code> line, and one
                <code>&lt;SortingViz client:visible /&gt;</code> tag where the widget belongs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tag lay">island</span>
            <div>
              <code>src/components/viz/SortingViz.tsx</code>
              <p>
                React. Owns controls, layout and colour. Contains no algorithm — it renders whatever
                the layer below hands it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tag lay">logic</span>
            <div>
              <code>src/lib/algorithms/sorting.ts</code>
              <p>
                Pure TypeScript, returns <code>SortStep[]</code>. This one module feeds both
                <code>/visualize/merge-sort/</code> and <code>/visualize/quick-sort/</code>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="rule">
          <strong>The rule that keeps this honest:</strong> nothing under <code>src/lib/</code> may
          import React. If you reach for <code>useState</code> in an algorithm file, that state
          belongs in the island. If you write an indexed <code>for</code> loop inside a component,
          it probably belongs in <code>lib/</code>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The ML and LLM material is the same shape one directory over:
          <code>src/lib/ml/</code> and <code>src/components/viz/ml/</code>, with
          <code>ml.css</code> alongside <code>viz.css</code>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="content">
        <h2><span class="num">04</span> Collections &amp; routing</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Collection</th><th>Directory</th><th>Route</th><th>Required</th><th>Optional</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                COLLECTIONS.map((c) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td><code>{c.name}</code></td>
                    <td>
                      <code>{c.dir}</code>
                      <br />
                      <span class="muted sm">{c.ext}</span>
                    </td>
                    <td><code>{c.route}</code></td>
                    <td class="sm">{c.required}</td>
                    <td class="sm muted">{c.optional}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          The visualization schema, verbatim — this is the contract your frontmatter has to satisfy:
        </p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{schemaCode}</code></pre>

        <p>
          <code>category</code> is required because <code>/visualize/</code> groups its cards with
          <code>Map.groupBy(all, (v) =&gt; v.data.category)</code>. A new category string creates a
          new section on that page automatically; reuse an existing string to file the card under it.
        </p>

        <p>And the whole route file it feeds:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{routeCode}</code></pre>

        <p>
          The index pages (<code>/blog/</code>, <code>/projects/</code>, <code>/visualize/</code>)
          and <code>/rss.xml</code> read the same accessors, so one new file shows up everywhere at
          once.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="patterns">
        <h2><span class="num">05</span> Two visualization patterns</h2>
        <p>
          Every widget on the site is one of these two. Decide which one fits the idea you are
          teaching before writing code — they lead to very different modules.
        </p>

        {
          PATTERNS.map((p) => (
            <div class="pattern">
              <h3>{p.name}</h3>
              <p>{p.what}</p>
              <p class="sm"><strong>Hooks:</strong> <code>{p.hooks}</code></p>
              <p class="sm muted"><strong>Used by:</strong> {p.where}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }

        <h3>How the trace pattern works</h3>
        <p>
          The algorithm module keeps a <code>Recorder</code> that pushes a full snapshot — the
          array, the pointers, the counters, a one-line <code>message</code> and a
          <code>CodeCursor</code> — at every interesting line. The component never runs the
          algorithm incrementally; it renders <code>steps[index]</code>.
        </p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{playerCode}</code></pre>
        <p class="sm muted">
          <code>usePlayer</code> returns <code>steps, step, index, last, playing, speedIdx,
          setSpeedIdx, load, toggle, seek</code>. Speeds are steps per second, 0.5 → 64.
          <code>PlayerControls</code> adds the transport buttons, a scrubber, the speed select, an
          <code>aria-live</code> caption and arrow-key / space shortcuts once focused.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="codepanel">
        <h2><span class="num">06</span> The code panel</h2>
        <p>
          This is what makes these pages teach rather than merely animate: the algorithm's source
          sits beside the picture, the executing line is highlighted, and the variables in scope are
          listed underneath with anything that just changed outlined. The reader can see
          <em>which line</em> produced the change.
        </p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{listingCode}</code></pre>
        <p>
          <code>listing()</code> in <code>src/lib/code.ts</code> dedents the source, strips the
          <code>//@ name</code> markers and records which line indices each name tags.
          <code>tokenize()</code> does a small hand-rolled syntax highlight, so the panel reads as
          code without shipping a highlighter to the browser.
        </p>
        <ul class="checks">
          <li><strong>Markers, not line numbers</strong> — the trace survives reformatting.</li>
          <li><strong>Repeat a marker</strong> on two lines to highlight a wrapped statement as one unit.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Keep display lines to 46 columns.</strong> The panel is a fixed column
            (<code>minmax(23rem, 0.9fr)</code>); longer lines clip.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Several operations?</strong> Export one listing per operation (build / query /
            update), pass a map, and let <code>cursor.listing</code> pick. Variables are only diffed
            within the same listing.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The listing is display code.</strong> Write the clearest version, not the traced
            implementation — it may differ in structure as long as it doesn't lie about what the
            step does.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="determinism">
        <h2><span class="num">07</span> Hydration rules</h2>
        <p>
          Islands render <em>twice</em>: once on the build machine into static HTML, once in the
          browser when React takes over. If the two disagree, React discards the server markup and
          you get a flicker or a console error. Three rules prevent it.
        </p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{determinismCode}</code></pre>
        <ol class="big-list">
          <li>
            <strong>No <code>Math.random()</code> in render.</strong> Anything random that is visible
            on first paint comes from the seeded <code>rng()</code> (mulberry32) in
            <code>src/lib/ml/math.ts</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Round every number that reaches markup</strong> with <code>pct()</code> /
            <code>r1()</code> from <code>src/components/viz/ml/num.ts</code>. An unrounded float can
            serialize as <code>127.371147280916</code> on one side and
            <code>127.37114728091598</code> on the other.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No <code>Date.now()</code> and no <code>window</code> during render.</strong>
            Touch the DOM in <code>useEffect</code> only. Canvas code that reads CSS variables uses
            <code>useThemeTick()</code> + <code>cssColor()</code> to re-read them when the theme
            flips.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section id="design">
        <h2><span class="num">08</span> Styling &amp; tokens</h2>
        <p>
          <code>src/styles/global.css</code> defines every colour as a CSS variable three times: a
          light <code>:root</code> block, a <code>@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)</code> block
          guarded by <code>:root:not([data-theme='light'])</code>, and an explicit
          <code>:root[data-theme='dark']</code> block for the manual toggle. Never hard-code a hex
          value in a component — use the token and dark mode comes free.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Token family</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><code>--bg --surface --surface-2 --border</code></td>
                <td>Backgrounds and dividers</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>--text --text-2 --text-3</code></td>
                <td>Primary / secondary / muted text</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>--role-compare --role-change --role-done --role-idle</code></td>
                <td>Step roles: looking at · writing or mismatch · settled · untouched</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>--cat-1 --cat-2 --cat-3</code></td>
                <td>
                  Series identity. There is deliberately no 4th — beyond three, distinguish by shape,
                  a direct label or faceting
                </td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>--seq-0</code> … <code>--seq-7</code></td>
                <td>Magnitude ramp (blue, light → dark)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>--div-neg --div-mid --div-pos</code></td>
                <td>Signed values: weights, activations, gradients</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="rule">
          Always pair a colour with a legend entry or a text label. The palette is validated for
          colour-blind safety in both themes, not for standing alone.
        </p>

        <h3>Class vocabulary</h3>
        <p>
          <code>viz.css</code> and <code>ml/ml.css</code> already carry the shared shapes. Reuse them
          instead of writing new CSS:
        </p>
        <ul class="chips">
          <li><code>.viz</code> outer card</li>
          <li><code>.viz-split</code> picture + code</li>
          <li><code>.viz-row</code> control row</li>
          <li><code>.viz-slider</code></li>
          <li><code>.viz-seg</code> segmented buttons</li>
          <li><code>.viz-cells</code> / <code>.viz-cell</code></li>
          <li><code>.viz-stats</code></li>
          <li><code>.viz-legend</code></li>
          <li><code>.viz-verdict</code></li>
          <li><code>.viz-label</code></li>
          <li><code>.viz-svg</code></li>
          <li><code>.btn</code> / <code>.input</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="sm muted">
          Shared React pieces: <code>&lt;Stat&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;PlayerControls&gt;</code>,
          <code>&lt;CodePanel&gt;</code>, and for ML <code>&lt;Heatmap&gt;</code> +
          <code>&lt;ScaleLegend&gt;</code> (sequential or diverging matrices with tooltips and
          masking) and <code>&lt;LineChart&gt;</code> (loss curves with a hover crosshair and an
          optional log scale).
        </p>

        <h3>Width</h3>
        <p>
          Inside <code>.prose</code>, direct children are capped at <code>44rem</code> for
          readability. Islands escape that automatically, because Astro wraps them in a
          <code>display:contents</code> <code>&lt;astro-island&gt;</code> element. For a wide
          <em>Astro</em> component, wrap it in <code>&lt;div class="wide"&gt;</code> — that is how
          the project pages make <code>DemoFrame</code> full width.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="add-viz">
        <h2><span class="num">09</span> Add a visualization</h2>
        <p class="recipe-note">
          Three new files, zero edits to existing ones. Count-Min Sketch as the worked example.
        </p>

        <h3><span class="step">1</span> The algorithm</h3>
        <p>
          <code>src/lib/algorithms/countMinSketch.ts</code> — plain TypeScript. Shared helpers
          (hashes, matrix maths) go in <code>hash.ts</code> / <code>ml/math.ts</code>. If you want
          the code panel, export a <code>listing()</code> and give every step a
          <code>CodeCursor</code>.
        </p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{algoCode}</code></pre>

        <h3><span class="step">2</span> The island</h3>
        <p>
          <code>src/components/viz/CountMinSketchViz.tsx</code> — default-export a React component.
          Import <code>./viz.css</code> and reuse its classes so the widget matches every other one
          on the site.
        </p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{islandCode}</code></pre>

        <h3><span class="step">3</span> The page</h3>
        <p>
          <code>src/content/visualizations/count-min-sketch.mdx</code> — the file name is the URL.
          Import the component with an explicit <code>.tsx</code> extension and a relative path, and
          mount it with <code>client:visible</code>.
        </p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{mdxCode}</code></pre>

        <p>
          That is all. <code>/visualize/count-min-sketch/</code> now exists, a card appears under
          "Probabilistic data structures" on <code>/visualize/</code>, and the sitemap picks it up.
          Run <code>npm run check</code> to type-check the frontmatter against the schema.
        </p>

        <h3>Placing the code panel</h3>
        <pre class="code"><code>{splitCode}</code></pre>
        <p class="sm muted">
          Below <code>64rem</code> the split collapses to one column, so the picture has to work
          stacked above the code.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="add-blog">
        <h2><span class="num">10</span> Add a blog post</h2>
        <p>
          One file: <code>src/content/blog/my-post.mdx</code> (or <code>.md</code> if you don't need
          components).
        </p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{blogCode}</code></pre>
        <p>
          It appears at <code>/blog/my-post/</code>, at the top of <code>/blog/</code> if its
          <code>pubDate</code> is the newest, and in <code>/rss.xml</code>.
          <code>draft: true</code> keeps it visible in <code>npm run dev</code> while hiding it from
          the production build — the filter lives in <code>lib/content.ts</code>, so it applies to
          the index, the route and the feed at once.
        </p>
        <p class="sm muted">
          A post can mount an island exactly like a visualization page does; MDX is MDX. That is the
          cheapest way to put one interactive figure inside an essay.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="add-project">
        <h2><span class="num">11</span> Add a project</h2>
        <p>One file: <code>src/content/projects/my-app.mdx</code>.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code>{projectCode}</code></pre>
        <dl class="steps">
          <div>
            <dt><code>status</code></dt>
            <dd>
              <code>live</code> · <code>wip</code> · <code>archived</code>, mapped to the eyebrow
              label by <code>STATUS_LABEL</code> in <code>lib/content.ts</code>.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>demo.embed</code></dt>
            <dd>
              <code>true</code> renders <code>DemoFrame</code> — a sandboxed, lazily-loaded iframe
              with a fake browser chrome bar — above the prose. The deployed app
              <strong>must allow framing</strong>: no <code>X-Frame-Options: DENY</code>, no
              restrictive <code>frame-ancestors</code>. If it can't be framed, set
              <code>embed: false</code>; the "Open live demo" button renders from
              <code>demo.url</code> either way.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt><code>featured</code></dt>
            <dd>Sorts to the top of <code>/projects/</code>, ahead of the date ordering.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="sm muted">
          For a demo that runs entirely in the browser, skip the iframe: write it as a component
          under <code>src/components/</code> and mount it in the MDX body with
          <code>client:visible</code>, the same as a visualization.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="gotchas">
        <h2><span class="num">12</span> Gotchas checklist</h2>
        <ul class="checks">
          <li>
            Component imports in MDX need the <strong>explicit <code>.tsx</code> extension</strong>
            and a relative path: <code>'../../components/viz/Foo.tsx'</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Forget <code>client:visible</code> and the widget renders as dead static HTML — no
            error, just nothing happens when you click it.
          </li>
          <li>Every island needs a <strong>default export</strong>.</li>
          <li>
            Anything random or time-based on first render breaks hydration. Seed it
            (<code>rng()</code>) or freeze it (a fixed initial array), and randomize on click.
          </li>
          <li>Round floats that reach markup with <code>pct()</code> / <code>r1()</code>.</li>
          <li>
            <code>category</code> is required on visualizations, and a new string silently creates a
            new heading on <code>/visualize/</code> — watch for a typo splitting one group in two.
          </li>
          <li>
            Dates are <code>z.coerce.date()</code>: unquoted <code>2026-10-01</code> is fine, an
            invalid date fails the build.
          </li>
          <li>Code-panel lines over <strong>46 columns</strong> get clipped.</li>
          <li>
            New colours belong in <code>global.css</code> as tokens, defined in <em>all three</em>
            theme blocks.
          </li>
          <li>
            Drafts are only hidden in production — verify with
            <code>npm run build &amp;&amp; npm run preview</code>, not <code>npm run dev</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Before deploying for real, set <code>site</code> in <code>astro.config.mjs</code> to your
            domain: canonical URLs, RSS and the sitemap all derive from it.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="map">
        <h2><span class="num">13</span> File map</h2>
        <pre class="code tree"><code>{treeCode}</code></pre>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>npm run dev</code></td><td>Dev server on :4321, drafts visible</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>npm run build</code></td><td>Static build into <code>dist/</code></td></tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>npm run preview</code></td>
                <td>Serve the production build (drafts hidden)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>npm run check</code></td>
                <td>Type-check <code>.astro</code>, <code>.ts</code>, <code>.tsx</code> and frontmatter</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <p class="foot muted">
        This page lives at <code>src/pages/architecture.astro</code>. It is not linked from the nav
        and is excluded from the sitemap and search engines, but it <em>is</em> part of the
        production build — delete the file to remove it.
      </p>
